## Why This Is Asked
Tests advanced 128-bit bit-twiddling, boundary shifts, and correctness under rotation.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values represented as hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Rotation across a 64-bit boundary
- Handling n in [0,127] with minimal branching in production

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  n &= 127; // modulo 128
  if (n < 64){
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  } else {
    unsigned k = n - 64;
    uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
    uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you make it fully branchless with masks?
- How would you adapt for different word sizes or endianness?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a robust, branchless 128-bit rotation, ensuring correct cross-boundary behavior and emphasis on manipulating split 64-bit halves.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across hi/lo halves
- Branchless implementation via masks and shifts
- Edge cases: n = 0, n = 64, n = 127

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
  n %= 128;
  uint128 res;
  if (n == 0) return x; // illustrative intent; prefer mask-based approach to avoid branches
  // Branchless approach would compute both paths and select via masks
  if (n < 64) {
    res.lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    res.hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
  } else {
    unsigned m = n - 64;
    res.lo = (x.hi << m) | (x.lo >> (64 - m));
    res.hi = (x.lo << m) | (x.hi >> (64 - m));
  }
  return res;
}
```

